+# H-1: Public /health endpoints serve frozen snapshot (staleness guard needed)
+
+- **Agent**: human-eye (Job 478633, cycle-19, 2026-10-29T00:57+08)
+- **Repo context**: Supremeai-bd/supremeai (private) + supremeai-open (public bridge)
+- **Severity**: P2 (monitoring blind spot; not user-facing outage)
+
+## Evidence
+
+Two independent Render services return a health JSON whose `timestamp` is frozen:
+
+| Endpoint | Frozen timestamp | Observed |
+|---|---|---|
+| `GET https://supremeai-mcp-tower.onrender.com/health` | `2026-10-24T11:41:20.752Z` | ms-identical across 15 hourly reads (2026-10-28 09:57 → 23:57 +08) |
+| `GET https://supremeai-api-ulw0.onrender.com/health` | `2026-10-24T11:40:55.206300+00:00` | ms-identical across the same 15 reads |
+
+- Both timestamps are ~4 days stale at observation time and are 25 seconds apart,
+  indicating a synchronized freeze around 2026-10-24T11:41Z on both services.
+- Status fields served from the same snapshot: tower `status:"degraded"` 28/29,
+  api `status:"healthy"`, `total_keys:51`, `chat_ready:true`, fleet 4/4 — all as-of Oct-24,
+  not live.
+- Contrast (same session, same day): MCP tool `health_full_sweep` on the Tower returns
+  **live** per-provider `checkedAt` values (e.g. `2026-10-28T16:58:51Z`, duration 2890ms,
+  incidents:[]) — so the sweep engine and providers are healthy; only the public HTTP
+  `/health` route serves a stale cached object.
+- The freeze also spans two different snapshot generations (earlier reads on 2026-10-28
+  morning showed a different frozen value `21:57:38.375Z`), proving a repeating
+  refresh-never-happens pattern, not a one-off.
+- Browser heartbeat corroborates: `[Heartbeat] Could not reach: …/health` seen during a
+  Render cold-start window (see companion auth-UX report), then recovers.
+
+## Root-cause hypothesis (fixer lane to confirm)
+
+The `/health` HTTP route returns a snapshot object produced by a background health-check
+loop that stopped updating on 2026-10-24 ~11:41 UTC (scheduler wedged or the route reads a
+cache that is never invalidated). Two services freezing within ~25s of each other suggests
+a shared deploy or a common health-job library version deployed that day.
+
+## Smallest safe fix
+
+1. On both services, make `GET /health` compute freshness at response time:
+   if `now - snapshot.timestamp > N minutes (suggest 5)`, return
+   `status:"stale"` + `snapshotAgeMs` (fail-visible) instead of the stale `healthy/degraded`.
+2. Restart/re-schedule the background health loop; add a watchdog that logs when the loop
+   hasn't ticked in >N minutes.
+3. No response-shape break: keep all existing keys; only `status` may become `"stale"`.
+
+## Regression test
+
+- Unit: fake clock — snapshot older than N minutes ⇒ `/health` returns `status:"stale"`.
+- Unit: fresh snapshot ⇒ current status preserved byte-for-byte keys.
+- Integration: after 2 loop ticks, `timestamp` advances; after freezing the loop, route
+  reports `stale` within N+1 minutes.
+
+## Why
+
+Monitoring/alerting and the human-eye QA lane itself consume these endpoints; a 4-day-old
+"healthy" snapshot masks real outages and false-advertises fleet state (51/51 keys,
+4/4 accounts are Oct-24 values). MCP `health_full_sweep` proves live checking is possible
+in the same codebase, so the HTTP route is the only broken surface.
+
+## Alternatives rejected
+
+- **Cache-busting query params / CDN purge**: freeze persists across 15 untruncated reads
+  and is server-side (sweep is fresh; route is stale) — not an HTTP-cache problem.
+- **Replace endpoints with pass-through to health_full_sweep on every request**: heavier
+  and slower; sweep takes ~2.9s and hammers providers — staleness guard + loop fix is smaller.
+- **Client-side staleness warnings only (consumers compute age)**: pushes the fix to every
+  consumer; server-side fail-visible status fixes it once for all consumers.
